DOMAIN: Cosmopolitanism, Perpetual Peace, and Contemporary Political Theory
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-03-13
PAPERS_FOUND: 18 total (High: 9, Medium: 7, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers the contemporary reception of Kant's "Toward Perpetual Peace" (1795) and
its relationship to the philosophy of history. The text's "guarantee article" — which holds
that nature mechanically produces the conditions for peace through commerce, mutual interest,
and the republican constitution — connects Kant's teleological philosophy of history to
normative political theory: peace is both historically achievable through natural mechanisms
and morally obligatory. Contemporary scholarship debates whether this guarantee commits Kant
to providence or can be read naturalistically, and whether the federation Kant envisions
(foedus pacificum) is a voluntary league of free states or entails a stronger cosmopolitan
institutional order.

Contemporary cosmopolitanism divides into two broad camps: those who follow Kant toward
minimal hospitality norms and a voluntary federation of sovereign states (closer to the text),
and those who press toward stronger global institutional reform (Pogge, Beitz, Habermas).
Rawls's "Law of Peoples" (1999) is typically read as a Kantian-inflected alternative that
stops short of global distributive justice, drawing critique from cosmopolitan theorists who
argue Rawls concedes too much to state sovereignty. Habermas's postnational constellation
reconstructs Kantian cosmopolitanism through deliberative democracy, arguing for a three-tier
global order, but critics question whether this faithfully extends or departs from Kant.

A third major debate concerns race and colonialism in Kant. The revisionist scholarship of
Kleingeld, Bernasconi, Lu-Adler, Marwah, and Gani has shown that Kant's race theory
(especially pre-1790s writings) is entangled with and in tension with his cosmopolitan
hospitality norms. Kleingeld argues that Kant underwent a genuine change of mind on race in
the mid-1790s; Lu-Adler and Marwah dispute this revisionist narrative, arguing that Kant's
racial hierarchy was never genuinely abandoned and that his philosophy of history constructs
progress in inherently Eurocentric terms. This debate directly affects assessments of
whether Kant's cosmopolitan ideals are philosophically redeemable or fundamentally compromised.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
The guarantee article in "Perpetual Peace" is the pivotal link between Kant's philosophy of
history and his normative political theory: nature's purposive design is supposed to ensure
that the empirical conditions for perpetual peace are gradually realized regardless of human
moral motivation. This makes Domain 6 essential for understanding how the teleological
framework of the philosophy of history (Domain 2-3) generates concrete political prescriptions
and how contemporary political theorists have appropriated, developed, or contested this
connection.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The contemporary literature on Kantian cosmopolitanism rarely engages substantively with the
guarantee article as a philosophy-of-history thesis rather than as a political claim.
Conversely, the philosophy-of-history literature rarely engages with the normative political
theory. The debate on race and colonialism, while increasingly rich, has not yet been
systematically integrated with analyses of how the philosophy of history's teleological
framework shapes Kant's assessments of non-European peoples. The Lu-Adler/Kleingeld debate
remains live; several entries are INCOMPLETE due to missing abstracts.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The synthesis should treat the guarantee article as the theoretical hinge connecting domains:
trace how nature's providential role in "Perpetual Peace" extends the unsocial sociability
mechanism from the "Idea for a Universal History" into a theory of international politics,
then assess whether the contemporary reception (Habermas, Pogge, Rawls) is continuous with
or departs from Kant's own teleological framing. The race/colonialism scholarship should be
treated as a challenge to the coherence of the cosmopolitan ideal, not merely a biographical
footnote.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Minimal federation / hospitality reading: [5 papers] — Kant's cosmopolitanism entails
  only a voluntary league of states and minimal hospitality rights, not a global state
- Stronger cosmopolitan institutionalism: [4 papers] — Pogge, Habermas, Beitz argue for
  robust global institutions; use Kant as a foundation but go beyond him
- Race/colonialism critique: [5 papers] — Kleingeld revisionism; Lu-Adler/Marwah counter-
  revisionism; Gani on erasure of race; Eberl on complexity
- Rawls as foil: [2 papers] — Law of Peoples as inadequate Kantian alternative
- Guarantee/providence scholarship: [2 papers] — Molloy on political theology; guarantee
  article connecting history and normative politics
====================================================================
}

@book{kleingeld2011kant,
  author = {Kleingeld, Pauline},
  title = {Kant and Cosmopolitanism},
  year = {2011},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9781139015486},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kleingeld argues that Kant's cosmopolitanism underwent a radical transformation
  in the mid-1790s, from an earlier endorsement of a racial hierarchy and European colonialism
  to a genuinely universalist cosmopolitan position. She reconstructs Kant's views on the
  relationship between cosmopolitanism and patriotism, international federation, cultural
  pluralism, race, and global economic justice by contrasting them with contemporaries
  (Fichte, Cloots, Forster, Hegewisch).

  RELEVANCE: This is the central scholarly reconstruction of Kant's cosmopolitanism, arguing
  that "Perpetual Peace" marks a turning point in Kant's political philosophy rather than a
  stable position. It directly addresses the relationship between the philosophy of history
  (the guarantee article, nature's role) and normative cosmopolitan theory, and anchors the
  race/colonialism debate.

  POSITION: Revisionist interpretation of Kant's cosmopolitanism as a developing and
  ultimately consistent universalism.
  },
  keywords = {cosmopolitanism, perpetual-peace, race, federation, High},
  abstract = {This is the first comprehensive account of Kant's cosmopolitanism, highlighting its moral, political, legal, economic, cultural and psychological aspects. Contrasting Kant's views with those of his German contemporaries and relating them to current debates, Pauline Kleingeld sheds new light on texts that have been hitherto neglected or underestimated. In clear and carefully argued discussions, she shows that Kant's philosophical cosmopolitanism underwent a radical transformation in the mid 1790s and that the resulting theory is philosophically stronger than is usually thought. Using the work of figures such as Fichte, Cloots, Forster, Hegewisch, Wieland and Novalis, Kleingeld analyses Kant's arguments regarding the relationship between cosmopolitanism and patriotism, the importance of states, the ideal of an international federation, cultural pluralism, race, global economic justice and the psychological feasibility of the cosmopolitan ideal. In doing so, she reveals a broad spectrum of positions in cosmopolitan theory that are relevant to current discussions of cosmopolitanism.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{kleingeld2004approaching,
  author = {Kleingeld, Pauline},
  title = {Approaching Perpetual Peace: Kant's Defence of a League of States and his Ideal of a World Federation},
  journal = {European Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {2004},
  volume = {12},
  number = {3},
  pages = {304--325},
  doi = {10.1111/j.0966-8373.2004.00212.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kleingeld argues that Kant's political texts articulate two different ideals
  in tension — a league of free states (foedus pacificum) that is realistically achievable
  and a world federation (civitas gentium) that is the regulative ideal but currently
  utopian. She contends that Kant endorses the league as an approximation to the federation,
  not as a permanent substitute, and that interpreters who treat them as competing views
  misread the texts.

  RELEVANCE: This article directly addresses the key interpretive question of whether Kant's
  cosmopolitanism requires robust global institutions (world state or federation) or only
  minimal voluntary cooperation among sovereigns. It is crucial for situating the guarantee
  article: nature works through the league toward the eventual federation.

  POSITION: Defense of Kant's institutional cosmopolitanism as requiring more than a minimal
  voluntary league, though less than a world state.
  },
  keywords = {perpetual-peace, federation, league-of-states, cosmopolitanism, High},
  abstract = {References are to Kants Gesammelte Schriften, edited under the auspices of the Royal Prussian Academy of Sciences (Berlin: Walter de Gruyter, 1902–). References to the Critique of Pure Reason will list the page numbers of the A and B editions. Translations are my own. Abbreviations: C1=Critique of Pure Reason; IUH=Idea for a Universal History from a Cosmopolitan Point of View; MM=Metaphysics of Morals; PP=Perpetual Peace; Rel=Religion within the Limits of Reason Alone; TP=On the Common Saying: This May Be True in Theory But It Does Not Apply in Practice.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{kleingeld1998cosmopolitanlaw,
  author = {Kleingeld, Pauline},
  title = {Kant's Cosmopolitan Law: World Citizenship for a Global Order},
  journal = {Kantian Review},
  year = {1998},
  volume = {2},
  pages = {72--90},
  doi = {10.1017/s1369415400000200},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kleingeld examines Kant's third level of public right — cosmopolitan right —
  analyzing its addressees (individuals as world citizens), content (the right to hospitality),
  justification (the original community of the earth's surface), and institutionalization.
  She argues that Kant's concept of world citizenship is neither merely metaphorical nor
  dependent on an ideal world state, but constitutes a genuine third tier of public right.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the foundational analysis of cosmopolitan right as distinct from both
  domestic civil right and international (inter-state) right. This three-level structure
  (state, international, cosmopolitan) connects directly to "Perpetual Peace"'s normative
  architecture and the guarantee article's role.

  POSITION: Systematic reconstruction of Kantian cosmopolitan right as a genuine normative
  category grounded in the original possession of the earth's surface.
  },
  keywords = {cosmopolitan-right, hospitality, world-citizenship, High},
  abstract = {In debates over the conditions for a just world order, one hears frequent appeals to Kant's call for states to unite in a federation. Given the force of Kant's arguments and their influence on the shape of such institutions as the League of Nations and the United Nations, this is certainly justified. But an essential part of what Kant saw as necessary for a global legal order is usually neglected. What is overlooked is Kant's emphasis on the status of individuals under what he calls ‘cosmopolitan law’. Cosmopolitan law is concerned not with the interaction between states, but with the status of individuals in their dealings with states of which they are not citizens. Moreover, it is concerned with the status of individuals as human beings, rather than as citizens of states. In Kant's political theory, cosmopolitan law ( Weltbürgerrecht ) is the third category of public law, in addition to constitutional law and international law. Its core is what Kant calls a right to hospitality. He argues that states and individuals have the right to attempt to establish relations with other states and their citizens, but not a right to enter foreign territory. States have the right to refuse visitors, but not violently, and not if it leads to their destruction. This implies an obligation to refrain from imperialist intrusions and to provide safe haven for refugees.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{kleingeld2007race,
  author = {Kleingeld, Pauline},
  title = {{KANT'S SECOND THOUGHTS ON RACE}},
  journal = {The Philosophical Quarterly},
  year = {2007},
  volume = {57},
  number = {229},
  pages = {573--592},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1467-9213.2007.498.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kleingeld argues, on textual grounds, that Kant radically changed his views
  on race between the 1780s and 1790s. The mid-1780s race texts defend a racial hierarchy
  with whites at the top; the 1790s political writings (especially "Perpetual Peace" and the
  Metaphysics of Morals) reflect a rejection of this hierarchy, consistent with Kant's
  universalist moral theory. This represents a genuine philosophical development rather than
  mere inconsistency.

  RELEVANCE: This article initiated the major contemporary debate about whether Kant's
  cosmopolitanism is compromised by his racial theory. It bears directly on the project's
  questions about the relationship between the philosophy of history's teleological framework
  and Kant's assessments of non-European peoples.

  POSITION: Revisionist: Kant did change his mind on race, making his mature cosmopolitanism
  more defensible than critics who treat his race views as fixed throughout his career.
  },
  keywords = {race, colonialism, revisionism, cosmopolitanism, High},
  abstract = {During the 1780s, as Kant was developing his universalistic moral theory, he published texts in which he defended the superiority of whites over non‐whites. Whether commentators see this as evidence of inconsistent universalism or of consistent inegalitarianism, they generally assume that Kant's position on race remained stable during the 1780s and 1790s. Against this standard view, I argue on the basis of his texts that Kant radically changed his mind. I examine his 1780s race theory and his hierarchical conception of the races, and subsequently address the question of the significance of these views, especially in the light of Kant's own ethical theory. I then show that during the 1790s Kant restricts the role of the concept of race, and drops his hierarchical account of the races in favour of a more genuinely egalitarian and cosmopolitan view.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{kleingeld2014colonialism,
  author = {Kleingeld, Pauline},
  title = {Kant's Second Thoughts on Colonialism},
  booktitle = {Kant and Colonialism},
  year = {2014},
  pages = {43--67},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi = {10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199669622.003.0003},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kleingeld argues that Kant endorsed and justified European colonialism until
  the early 1790s, with this endorsement deeply connected to his racial hierarchy. His
  subsequent criticism of colonialism in "Perpetual Peace" and the Metaphysics of Morals is
  related to his changing views on race: as he moved toward racial egalitarianism (per her
  revisionist thesis), he correspondingly moved toward anti-colonialism.

  RELEVANCE: Connects the race/cosmopolitanism debate directly to the philosophy of history:
  if Kant's 1780s teleological framework presupposed racial hierarchy, then the progression
  narrative in works like "Idea for a Universal History" is implicated. Lu-Adler and Marwah
  contest Kleingeld's conclusion here.

  POSITION: Revisionist: Kant's anti-colonialism in the 1790s reflects genuine philosophical
  change, not inconsistency or mere superficial gesture.
  },
  keywords = {colonialism, race, revisionism, perpetual-peace, High},
  abstract = {Abstract Kant is widely regarded as a fierce critic of colonialism. In Toward Perpetual Peace and the Metaphysics of Morals, for example, he forcefully condemns European conduct in the colonies as a flagrant violation of the principles of right. His earlier views on colonialism have not yet received much detailed scrutiny, however. This essay argues that Kant actually endorsed and justified European colonialism until the early 1790s. The chapter shows that Kant’s initial endorsement and his subsequent criticism of colonialism are closely related to his changing views on race, because his endorsement of a racial hierarchy plays a crucial role in his justification of European colonialism. He gave up both in the mid 1790s while he was developing his legal and political philosophy, and he adopted a more egalitarian version of the cosmopolitan relationship among peoples.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{luadler2022slavery,
  author = {Lu-Adler, Huaping},
  title = {Kant and Slavery---Or Why He Never Became a Racial Egalitarian},
  journal = {Critical Philosophy of Race},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {10},
  number = {2},
  pages = {263--294},
  doi = {10.5325/critphilrace.10.2.0263},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lu-Adler challenges Kleingeld's revisionist narrative, arguing that Kant's
  1790s texts (including "Perpetual Peace") do not constitute a genuine rejection of his
  racial hierarchy. When contextualized, passages about slavery either do not concern
  modern race-based chattel slavery or treat it as a cautionary tale for European labor
  practices. Kant's philosophy of history — which depicted "Negroes" as natural slaves —
  shaped what he could and could not say about slavery; the humanity of the enslaved was
  never part of his moral reasoning about the topic.

  RELEVANCE: Directly challenges the view that Kant's mature cosmopolitanism is separated
  from his racial theory. If Lu-Adler is right, the teleological narrative of the philosophy
  of history is structurally tied to racial hierarchy in ways that undermine the universalist
  cosmopolitan ideal. This is a major challenge to rehabilitative readings of Kant's political
  philosophy.

  POSITION: Counter-revisionist: Kant's racial views were never genuinely abandoned; the
  philosophy of history's teleological framework treats racial hierarchy as part of the
  natural progression.
  },
  keywords = {race, colonialism, counter-revisionism, philosophy-of-history, High},
  abstract = {Abstract:According to an oft-repeated narrative, while Kant maintained racist views through the 1780s, he changed his mind in the 1790s. Pauline Kleingeld introduced this narrative based on passages from Kant’s Metaphysics of Morals (1797) and “Toward Perpetual Peace” (1795). On her reading, Kant categorically condemned chattel slavery (and colonialism) in those texts, which meant that he became more racially egalitarian. But the passages involving slavery, once contextualized, either do not concern modern, race-based chattel slavery or at best suggest that Kant mentioned it as a cautionary tale for labor practices in Europe. Overall, Kant never explicitly considered chattel slavery as a moral problem to be addressed on its own. Rather, he treated it primarily in terms of its function in human history. If he ended up expressing some qualms about its practices, it was likely because they threatened to deepen intra-European conflicts and undermine the prospect of perpetual peace. The humanity of the enslaved “Negroes” was never part of the reasoning. This was not a casual oversight on Kant’s part. It reflects the complexity of his philosophical system: everything he did or did not say about chattel slavery begins to make sense once we connect his philosophy of history and his depiction of “Negroes” as natural slaves.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{marwah2022white,
  author = {Marwah, Inder S.},
  title = {White Progress: Kant, Race and Teleology},
  journal = {Kantian Review},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {27},
  number = {4},
  pages = {615--634},
  doi = {10.1017/s1369415422000334},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Marwah argues that Kant's teleological philosophy of history is structured
  around a Eurocentric conception of progress in which "white" civilization represents the
  culmination of human development. Race is not merely incidental to the philosophy of history
  but shapes its teleological architecture: the stages of cultural development that Kant
  treats as universal are in fact modeled on European particularity.

  RELEVANCE: Directly links the race/colonialism debate to the teleological philosophy of
  history (Domains 1-3). Marwah's argument complicates the project's central inquiry: if
  the philosophy of history's progressivism is racially structured, then its connection
  to cosmopolitan hospitality norms is internally contradictory.

  POSITION: Counter-revisionist/critical: Kant's teleological framework is constitutively
  Eurocentric, not merely contingently associated with racist views in some texts.
  },
  keywords = {race, teleology, Eurocentrism, philosophy-of-history, High},
  abstract = {Abstract This article examines how Kant’s conceptualizations of natural history and teleological judgement shape his understanding of human difference and race. I argue that the teleological framework encasing Kant’s racial theory implies constraints on the capacity of non-whites to make moral progress. While commentators tend to approach Kant’s racial theory in relation to his political theory, his late-life cosmopolitanism, and his treatments (or non-treatments) of colonialism, empire and slavery, the problem I focus on here is that race is itself only intelligible in relation to a teleological natural history limiting certain races’ capacities to engage in humanity’s moral vocation.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{gani2017erasure,
  author = {Gani, J. K.},
  title = {The Erasure of Race: Cosmopolitanism and the Illusion of Kantian Hospitality},
  journal = {Millennium: Journal of International Studies},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {45},
  number = {3},
  pages = {425--446},
  doi = {10.1177/0305829817714064},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Gani argues that Kant's cosmopolitan hospitality norm in "Perpetual Peace"
  performs an erasure of race: it presents itself as universal while presupposing a racial
  framework that excludes non-European peoples from full hospitality rights. The cosmopolitan
  ideal, far from transcending racial hierarchies, depends on their suppression from view.

  RELEVANCE: Offers a critical international relations perspective on Kant's hospitality
  norms, complementing Kleingeld's philosophical analysis with attention to how the guarantee
  article's assumption that commerce and hospitality generate peace is racially coded.

  POSITION: Critical/postcolonial: Kant's cosmopolitan hospitality is an ideological
  construction that naturalizes European expansion under the guise of universal rights.
  },
  keywords = {race, hospitality, colonialism, cosmopolitanism, postcolonial, High},
  abstract = {This article explores three key arguments: Firstly, it seeks to demonstrate the contradictions and limits within Kantian hospitality, and its links to colonialism and practices of racialisation. The acclaimed universalism of Kant’s law of hospitality forecloses a discussion of its dualism, and erases the historical, racist context in which it was conceived. The prioritization of concept over conception allows Kant’s theory on race to be obscured from official discourse and framing of policies while it still courses through inherited perceptions and theories. Secondly, in making my case, I will be applying the notion of coloniality, coined by Aníbal Quijano and later developed by Walter Mignolo, to the existing but small body of critical discourse on Kant and race. Debates initiated on the peripheries of philosophy, law and anthropology in the 1990s have led the way in this regard. However, given the time that has elapsed, it is notable that their work has received little scrutiny in political theory and International Relations theory, and thus warrants renewed attention. I argue that the notion of coloniality provides a useful lens through which to do so, and a vehicle through which to apply those excavations to a contemporary context. Finally, the article explores the extent to which Kantian thought constitutes ‘modern’ cosmopolitanism, and draws attention to the inadvertently complicit role of second-generation cosmopolitans in the erasure of race from the study of Kant. The relationship between the collective erasure of race and racism in academia and European practice towards refugees and immigrants is briefly considered.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{molloy2017international,
  author = {Molloy, Sean},
  title = {Kant's International Relations},
  year = {2017},
  publisher = {University of Michigan Press},
  doi = {10.3998/mpub.5036715},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Molloy argues that "Perpetual Peace" and "Idea for a Universal History"
  cannot be fully understood without reference to the role that belief in God and Providence
  plays in Kant's critical philosophy. The guarantee article's appeal to nature as guarantor
  of peace is a form of political theology: Kant needs the God of the postulates of practical
  reason to make the guarantee stick. This reveals political-theological dimensions of Kant's
  thought that are obscured by purely secular readings.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the guarantee article and its relationship to Kant's
  philosophy of history and theology. Molloy's political-theological reading challenges
  both naturalistic interpretations (nature as mechanism) and purely moral interpretations
  (nature as regulative ideal) of the guarantee.

  POSITION: Political-theological: the guarantee article in "Perpetual Peace" depends on
  theistic commitments that link it to Kant's practical faith in God rather than to a
  secular teleology of nature.
  },
  keywords = {guarantee-article, providence, perpetual-peace, political-theology, Medium},
  abstract = {Why does Immanuel Kant (1724-1804) consistently invoke God and Providence in his most prominent texts relating to international politics? This question animates this study of one of the preeminent philosophers of modernity. In this wide ranging study, Se n Molloy proposes that texts such as Idea for a Universal History with Cosmopolitan Intent and Toward Perpetual Peace cannot be fully understood without reference to Kant's wider philosophical projects, and in particular the role that belief in God plays within critical philosophy and Kant's inquiries into anthropology, politics, and theology. The broader view that Molloy provides reveals the political-theological dimensions of Kant's thought as directly related to his attempts to find a new basis for metaphysics in the sacrifice of knowledge to make room for faith.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{finesmith2003habermas,
  author = {Fine, Robert and Smith, Will},
  title = {J{\"u}rgen Habermas's Theory of Cosmopolitanism},
  journal = {Constellations},
  year = {2003},
  volume = {10},
  number = {4},
  pages = {469--487},
  doi = {10.1046/j.1351-0487.2003.00348.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Fine and Smith critically assess Habermas's attempt to reconstruct Kant's
  cosmopolitanism through deliberative democracy and constitutional patriotism. They argue
  that Habermas faces two dilemmas: grounding normative cosmopolitanism without the
  Kantian moral metaphysics he rejects, and reconciling his cosmopolitan commitments with
  the national framework in which his deliberative democracy is embedded. "Thinking with
  Kant against Kant" (Apel's challenge) proves difficult for Habermas.

  RELEVANCE: The key secondary source for assessing the Habermasian reconstruction of
  Kantian cosmopolitanism. Bearing directly on how the philosophy of history's progressive
  teleology is or is not preserved in Habermas's postnational constellation, and whether
  the Kantian guarantee article has a functional equivalent in Habermas's discourse theory.

  POSITION: Critical assessment of Habermasian cosmopolitanism as both dependent on and in
  tension with its Kantian inheritance.
  },
  keywords = {Habermas, cosmopolitanism, deliberative-democracy, postnational, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract In this paper we explore the sustained and multifaceted attempt of Jürgen Habermas to reconstruct Kant's theory of cosmopolitan right for our own times. In a series of articles written in the post‐1989 period, Habermas has argued that the challenge posed both by the catastrophes of the twentieth century, and by social forces of globalization, has given new impetus to the idea of cosmopolitan justice that Kant first expressed. He recognizes that today we cannot simply repeat Kant's eighteenth‐century vision: that if we are to grapple with the complexities of present‐day problems, it is necessary to iron out certain inconsistencies in Kant's thinking, radicalize it where its break from the old order of nation‐states is incomplete, socialize it so as to draw out the connections between perpetual peace and social justice, and modernize it so as to comprehend the “differences both in global situation and conceptual framework that now separate us from him.” 1 His basic intuition, however, is that Kant's idea of cosmopolitan right is as relevant to our times as it was to Kant's own. If it was Kant's achievement to formulate the idea of cosmopolitanism in a modern philosophical form, Habermas takes up the challenge posed by Karl‐Otto Apel: to “think with Kant against Kant” in reconstructing this idea. What follows is a critical assessment of Habermas's response to this challenge. We focus here on the dilemmas he faces in grounding his normative commitment to cosmopolitan politics and in reconciling his cosmopolitanism with the national framework in which he developed his ideas of constitutional patriotism and deliberative democracy.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{pogge1992cosmopolitanism,
  author = {Pogge, Thomas W.},
  title = {Cosmopolitanism and Sovereignty},
  journal = {Ethics},
  year = {1992},
  volume = {103},
  number = {1},
  pages = {48--75},
  doi = {10.1086/293470},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Pogge argues for a version of institutional cosmopolitanism that requires
  dispersed sovereignty across multiple levels — local, national, and supranational — rather
  than a traditional state-centric international order. Human rights impose direct
  obligations on global institutions, not merely on states. Pogge distinguishes legal,
  moral, and institutional cosmopolitanism and argues for their convergence in a reformist
  program.

  RELEVANCE: Pogge's cosmopolitanism is the major contemporary institutional cosmopolitan
  alternative that claims Kantian ancestry while going well beyond Kant's own minimal
  federation. It serves as a reference point for assessing how far contemporary theory
  has departed from or developed Kant's own cosmopolitanism in "Perpetual Peace."

  POSITION: Institutional cosmopolitanism that argues for global institutional reform on
  grounds of human rights, deploying Kantian moral universalism while departing from
  Kant's state-centric politics.
  },
  keywords = {cosmopolitanism, global-justice, sovereignty, institutional, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@incollection{beitz2005cosmopolitanism,
  author = {Beitz, Charles R.},
  title = {Cosmopolitanism and Global Justice},
  booktitle = {Studies in Global Justice},
  year = {2005},
  pages = {11--27},
  publisher = {Springer-Verlag},
  doi = {10.1007/1-4020-3847-x_2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Beitz distinguishes moral cosmopolitanism (claims about the status of
  persons) from political cosmopolitanism (claims about global institutions) and argues
  that the former does not straightforwardly entail the latter. He defends an "international
  liberalism" that combines moral cosmopolitan commitments with attention to the constructive
  role of political institutions in specifying and implementing those commitments globally.

  RELEVANCE: Beitz's earlier "Political Theory and International Relations" (1979) applied
  Rawlsian principles globally, and this essay develops that project into a mature
  cosmopolitan theory. Together with Pogge, Beitz represents the cosmopolitan wing that
  reads Kant's moral universalism as requiring stronger global institutions than Kant himself
  endorsed.

  POSITION: Liberal cosmopolitanism that presses beyond Rawls's "Law of Peoples" toward
  global distributive justice on Kantian moral foundations.
  },
  keywords = {cosmopolitanism, global-justice, Rawls, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{nussbaum1997stoic,
  author = {Nussbaum, Martha C.},
  title = {Kant and Stoic Cosmopolitanism},
  journal = {Journal of Political Philosophy},
  year = {1997},
  volume = {5},
  number = {1},
  pages = {1--25},
  doi = {10.1111/1467-9760.00021},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Nussbaum traces the Stoic roots of Kant's cosmopolitanism and argues that
  the Kantian version both inherits and transforms the Stoic doctrine of concentric circles
  of loyalty. She defends a conception of "world citizenship" that grounds moral obligations
  to all humanity in our shared rational nature, while arguing that Kant's version lacks the
  Stoics' emphasis on emotional attachment across boundaries.

  RELEVANCE: Situates Kant's cosmopolitanism in the longue duree of the philosophical
  tradition and explicitly engages with "Perpetual Peace" and the categorical imperative as
  foundations for cosmopolitan obligations. Nussbaum's account represents a prominent
  contemporary defense of a Kantian-inspired cosmopolitanism in the analytic tradition.

  POSITION: Moral cosmopolitanism grounded in Kantian/Stoic rational universalism, with
  normative implications for global education and political obligation.
  },
  keywords = {cosmopolitanism, Stoicism, moral-universalism, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{doyle1986liberalism,
  author = {Doyle, Michael W.},
  title = {Liberalism and World Politics},
  journal = {American Political Science Review},
  year = {1986},
  volume = {80},
  number = {4},
  pages = {1151--1169},
  doi = {10.2307/1960861},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Doyle argues that Kant's "Perpetual Peace" anticipates the liberal peace
  theory: liberal democracies tend not to go to war with each other, creating a "zone of
  peace" among liberal states. This Kantian insight, Doyle contends, is empirically
  supported by historical patterns and provides a bridge between normative political theory
  and international relations scholarship.

  RELEVANCE: The "democratic peace" thesis is one of the most influential contemporary
  appropriations of Kantian cosmopolitanism in international relations. It grounds the
  guarantee article's claim that republican constitutions promote peace in empirical
  observation, mediating between Kant's philosophy of history and empirical political
  science.

  POSITION: Liberal internationalism that takes Kant's guarantee article (especially the
  link between republican constitutions and peace) as a theoretical foundation for an
  empirically testable thesis.
  },
  keywords = {democratic-peace, republican-constitution, liberal-internationalism, perpetual-peace, Medium},
  abstract = {Building on a growing literature in international political science, I reexamine the traditional liberal claim that governments founded on a respect for individual liberty exercise “restraint” and “peaceful intentions” in their foreign policy. I look at three distinct theoretical traditions of liberalism, attributable to three theorists: Schumpeter, a democratic capitalist whose explanation of liberal pacifism we often invoke; Machiavelli, a classical republican whose glory is an imperialism we often practice; and Kant, a liberal republican whose theory of internationalism best accounts for what we are. Despite the contradictions of liberal pacifism and liberal imperialism, I find, with Kant and other democratic republicans, that liberalism does leave a coherent legacy on foreign affairs. Liberal states are different. They are indeed peaceful. They are also prone to make war. Liberal states have created a separate peace, as Kant argued they would, and have also discovered liberal reasons for aggression, as he feared they might. I conclude by arguing that the differences among liberal pacifism, liberal imperialism, and Kant's internationalism are not arbitrary. They are rooted in differing conceptions of the citizen and the state.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{valdez2017notrace,
  author = {Valdez, In\'es},
  title = {It's Not about Race: Good Wars, Bad Wars, and the Origins of Kant's Anti-Colonialism},
  journal = {American Political Science Review},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {111},
  number = {4},
  pages = {819--834},
  doi = {10.1017/S0003055417000223},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Valdez argues, against Kleingeld, that Kant's late anti-colonialism is
  not grounded in a changed view on race but in a distinction between "good wars" and "bad
  wars" internal to his republican and legal framework. Kant opposes colonialism as a
  violation of cosmopolitan and juridical right, not because he abandoned racial hierarchy.
  This means that Kant's anti-colonialism is consistent with his retention of racial
  distinctions.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a third position in the Kleingeld vs. Lu-Adler/Marwah debate: Kant's
  anti-colonialism in "Perpetual Peace" can be explained without attributing a change in
  racial views, which complicates both the revisionist and counter-revisionist narratives.
  Directly bears on whether the cosmopolitan ideal is coherent or in internal tension.

  POSITION: Neither revisionist nor counter-revisionist: anti-colonialism is driven by
  juridical concerns independent of racial theory, leaving the racial hierarchy intact
  while anti-colonialism is endorsed.
  },
  keywords = {colonialism, race, perpetual-peace, anti-colonialism, Medium},
  abstract = {This article offers a new interpretation of Kant's cosmopolitanism and his anti-colonialism in Toward Perpetual Peace . Kant's changing position has been the subject of extensive debates that have, however, not recognized the central place of colonialism in the political, economic, and military debates in Europe in Kant's writings. Based on historical evidence not previously considered alongside Perpetual Peace , I suggest that Kant's leading concern at the time of writing is the negative effect of European expansionism and intra-European rivalry over colonial possessions on the possibility of peace in Europe. Because of the lack of affinity between colonial conflict and his philosophy of history, Kant must adjust his concept of antagonism to distinguish between war between particular dyads, in particular spaces, and with particular non-state actors. I examine the implications of this argument for Kant's system of Right and conclude that his anti-colonialism co-exists with hierarchical views of race.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{eberl2019barbarism,
  author = {Eberl, Oliver},
  title = {Kant on Race and Barbarism: Towards a More Complex View on Racism and Anti-Colonialism in Kant},
  journal = {Kantian Review},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {24},
  number = {3},
  pages = {385--413},
  doi = {10.1017/s1369415419000189},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Eberl argues that the relationship between Kant's legal theory and his
  race theory is more complex than either revisionists or counter-revisionists allow. Kant's
  race writings were not designed to ground moral judgments on racial hierarchy but to
  defend the unity of mankind against colonial prejudices. Kant's early critical stance
  toward colonial judgments of native peoples can be traced through his writings on race,
  even though those writings contain racist statements.

  RELEVANCE: Offers a nuanced third perspective on the race/colonialism debate, arguing
  that the opposition between "racist Kant" and "anti-colonialist Kant" is too binary.
  This nuance bears on how to assess the philosophical coherence of Kant's cosmopolitan
  hospitality norms.

  POSITION: Complexity view: Kant's racism and anti-colonialism are not simply contradictory
  but arise from different concerns (natural history vs. legal theory) that can coexist in
  tension.
  },
  keywords = {race, colonialism, legal-theory, complexity, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract Whether Kant’s late legal theory and his theory of race are contradictory in their account of colonialism has been a much-debated question that is also of highest importance for the evaluation of the Enlightenment’s contribution to Europe’s colonial expansion and the dispossession and enslavement of native and black peoples. This article discusses the problem by introducing the discourse on barbarism. This neglected discourse is the original and traditional European colonial vocabulary and served the justification of colonialism from ancient Greece throughout the Renaissance to the eighteenth century. Kant’s explicit rejection of this discourse and its prejudices reveals his early critical stance toward colonial judgements of native peoples even before he developed his legal theory. This development of his critical position can be traced in his writings on race: although he makes racist statements in these texts, his theory of race is not meant to ground moral judgements on ‘races’ or a racial hierarchy but to defend the unity of mankind under the given empirical reality of colonial hierarchies.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{huber2017earthdwellers,
  author = {Huber, Jakob},
  title = {Cosmopolitanism for Earth Dwellers: Kant on the Right to be Somewhere},
  journal = {Kantian Review},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {22},
  number = {1},
  pages = {1--25},
  doi = {10.1017/s1369415416000340},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Huber develops what he calls "grounded cosmopolitanism," arguing that
  Kant's cosmopolitan right is grounded in an "argument from earth dwellership": because
  we are corporeal agents who occupy a finite spherical earth, we have an original community
  in the earth's surface that generates the right to hospitality. This reading is
  "grounded" in contrast to purely rational-law derivations and provides an independent
  strand of cosmopolitan argument.

  RELEVANCE: Recasts the philosophical grounding of Kant's hospitality norms in "Perpetual
  Peace" — connecting them to embodiment and finite spatial existence rather than purely
  moral universalism. This bears on whether the guarantee article's claim that nature
  disperses peoples across the globe has normative implications for cosmopolitan right.

  POSITION: Revisionist reconstruction of Kant's cosmopolitan right as grounded in
  corporeal earth-dwelling rather than derived purely from moral or juridical principles.
  },
  keywords = {hospitality, cosmopolitan-right, earth-dwellership, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract The paper provides a systematic account of Kant’s ‘right to be somewhere’ as introduced in the Doctrine of Right. My claim is that Kant’s concern with the concurrent existence of a plurality of corporeal agents on the earth’s surface (to which the right speaks) occupies a rarely appreciated conceptual space in his mature political philosophy. In grounding a particular kind of moral relation that is ‘external’ (as located in bounded space) but not property-mediated, it provides us with a fundamentally new perspective on Kant’s cosmopolitanism, which I construe as a cosmopolitanism for ‘earth dwellers’.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{kleingeld2016compass,
  author = {Kleingeld, Pauline},
  title = {Kant's Moral and Political Cosmopolitanism},
  journal = {Philosophy Compass},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {11},
  number = {1},
  pages = {14--23},
  doi = {10.1111/phc3.12298},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kleingeld surveys Kant's three-tiered cosmopolitan theory, distinguishing
  moral cosmopolitanism (obligations to all rational beings), political cosmopolitanism
  (requirements of international law and federation), and cultural cosmopolitanism
  (promotion of cultural exchange). She argues that these three dimensions are inter-related
  and that Kant's cosmopolitanism is more ambitious than minimalist hospitality readings suggest.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most accessible overview of the scholarship on Kant's
  cosmopolitanism in its multiple dimensions, situating "Perpetual Peace" within Kant's
  broader political philosophy and connecting to contemporary debates about global justice.

  POSITION: Systematic overview defending an ambitious reading of Kantian cosmopolitanism
  as politically, morally, and culturally demanding.
  },
  keywords = {cosmopolitanism, overview, political-philosophy, Low},
  abstract = {Abstract In this essay, I first outline the contexts in which the idea of cosmopolitanism appears in Kant's moral and political philosophy. I then survey the three main debates regarding his political cosmopolitanism, namely, on the nature of the international federation he advocated, his theory of cosmopolitan right, and his views on colonialism and ‘race’, and I consider the relation between patriotism and cosmopolitanism in Kant's work. I subsequently discuss Kant's moral cosmopolitanism. Kant is widely held to be a defender of moral cosmopolitanism, but the terminology of world citizenship is in fact strikingly rare in Kant's writings on moral theory. I offer a two‐part explanation for why this is the case.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}
